# Context filter for go/Dockerfile.siblings, whose build context is tee/ — the shared parent
# of tee-node/ and extension-examples/ (see docker-compose.siblings.yaml).
#
# Why the filter has to live here, under this name: BuildKit looks for a per-Dockerfile
# ignore file named <dockerfile>.dockerignore next to the Dockerfile it was given, and uses
# it *instead of* the context-root .dockerignore — the two are never merged. Compose passes
# the dockerfile as extension-examples/activity-reward-extension/go/Dockerfile.siblings, so
# the file BuildKit reads is this one, and it happens to sit inside this repo. The
# alternative, a context-root .dockerignore, would have to be created at tee/.dockerignore:
# outside every repo, on every machine, and therefore not shippable. This is the only context
# filter this build can carry with it.
#
# This file does not have to survive its own patterns: BuildKit reads it alongside the
# Dockerfile, not out of the filtered context, so the deny-all below excludes it from the
# context without disabling it.
#
# It is also only honoured by BuildKit. The legacy builder (DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0) reads nothing
# but a context-root .dockerignore, i.e. nothing at all for this build — which is why
# Dockerfile.siblings additionally COPYs individual paths instead of the repo root. Neither
# half is sufficient alone: this file keeps credentials out of the context that is uploaded
# to the daemon, the narrow COPYs keep them out of the builder layers and the local build
# cache even when this file is not read.
#
# Every pattern below is relative to the context root (tee/), NOT to this file's directory.
# `*` does not cross `/`; `**/foo` matches foo at any depth. The last matching pattern wins,
# so order matters: a `!` re-inclusion must come after the exclusion it overrides.

# Deny by default. The context is a developer's shared parent directory: it holds sibling
# checkouts this build has no business reading (tee-proxy, e2e, other extensions), each with
# its own .env files, credentials, node_modules and build output. Only the two inputs the
# Dockerfile copies are added back, path by path — anything else that appears in tee/ later
# stays out without this file having to know about it.
*

# The sibling tee-node module, copied in full: it is the module being developed here.
!tee-node

# The extension's Go module: manifest plus its own source packages. Everything else in the
# repo stays excluded, notably .env, .env.<chain>, config/extension.env and
# config/proxy/*.toml (indexer DB credentials in a real working copy), and the generated
# go.work, whose `use` entries point at host paths that do not exist in the container.
!extension-examples
extension-examples/*
!extension-examples/activity-reward-extension
extension-examples/activity-reward-extension/*
!extension-examples/activity-reward-extension/go
extension-examples/activity-reward-extension/go/*
!extension-examples/activity-reward-extension/go/go.mod
!extension-examples/activity-reward-extension/go/go.sum
!extension-examples/activity-reward-extension/go/cmd
!extension-examples/activity-reward-extension/go/internal
!extension-examples/activity-reward-extension/go/pkg

# Re-excluded inside the trees added back above. A tee-node working copy has its own .env
# and its own build/editor state; none of it is an input to a Go build. Listed after the
# re-inclusions so these win.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.env
**/.DS_Store
**/node_modules/
**/.venv/
**/dist/
tee-node/.git
